sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch08-02> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The fundamental rule of cell division is that the incipient partition of a dividing cell takes the least possible area that can divide the given volume; Sachs’s rule of successive divisions at right angles is a subordinate case of this principle.";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A new cell wall has the least area that can enclose the given volume (Errera)";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "partition \\Sigma = \\arg\\min |\\Sigma| subject to dividing V in the given ratio";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VIII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (continued)”; topic “Sachs’s and Errera’s rules of division”. Scope: dividing cells, especially plant cells and blastomeres"
}
